sentences.l:
I-want-to-you to-tell of-him who in a-good (hour) girded sword:
that hill on it he-took lodging;
while that may-be the people of Moors and of the folk Christian,
the Poyo of my Çid so-it they-will-call by writ.

sentences.t:
quando vío el caboso que se tardava Minaya,
con todas sus yentes fizo una trasnochada;
dexó el Poyo, todo lo desenparava,

sentences.l:
when saw the excellent-one that himself was-delaying Minaya,
with all his folk he-made a night-march;
he-left the Poyo, all it he-was-abandoning,

When this done he-had, at end of three weeks
from Castiella come is Minaya,
two-hundred with him, who all gird swords;
not are in count, know-ye, the foot-troops.

sentences.t:
El Campeador fermoso sonrrisava:
“grado a Dios e a las sus vertudes santas;
mientra vos visquiéredes, bien me irá a mí, Minaya!”

sentences.l:
The Campeador handsomely was-smiling:
“thanks to God and to the his virtues holy;
while you may-live, well to-me it-will-go to me, Minaya!”

sentences.t:
Non lo tardó el que en buen ora nasco,
priso dozientos cavalleros escollechos a mano,
fizo una corrida la noch trasnochando;

sentences.l:
Not it delayed he who in good hour was-born,
he-took two-hundred knights chosen by hand,
he-made a raid the night night-marching;

sentences.t:
Con estas ganançias a la posada tornando se van.
todos son alegres, ganançias traen grandes;
plogo a mio Çid, e mucho a Álbar Fáñez.

sentences.l:
With these gains to the lodging returning themselves they-go.
all are glad, gains they-bring great;
it-pleased my Çid, and much Álbar Fáñez.

sentences.t:
Estonçes se mudó el Çid, al puerto de Alucat;
dent corre mio Çid a Huesa e a Mont Alván;
en aquessa corrida diez dias ovieron a morar.

sentences.l:
Then himself moved the Çid, to-the pass of Alucat;
from-there runs my Çid to Huesa and to Mont Alván;
in that raid ten days they-had to stay.

sentences.t:
llegaron las nuevas al co[m]de de Barçilona,
que mio Çid R[o]y Díaz quel corrié la tierra toda;
ovo grand pesar e tóvoslo a grand fonta.

sentences.l:
arrived the news to-the count of Barçilona,
that my Çid R[o]y Díaz that-to-him was-overrunning the land all;
he-had great grief and held-it-to-himself for great affront.
